  "final_text": "All four steps are done.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (id `90943e30-9a47-11e8-b64d-95841ca0b247`) covers the index `kibana_sample_data_logs`. Its time field is `timestamp`. The data view has 45 fields, including `response` (string, with a `response.keyword` subfield), `bytes`, `clientip`, `geo.*`, `url`, `@timestamp` and `utc_time`.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-bf85abbb Web logs' (id `53efb45e-ee32-40f0-9908-7a031e6f3a0b`) on that data view. It has two panels: a line chart of request count over time, and a bar chart of request count by `response.keyword`.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-bf85abbb 5xx errors' (id `79f79115-41cc-4bdc-a419-94c2fb609d1b`) as an Elasticsearch query rule. It runs every minute over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is above 10. It matches `response.keyword` from `\"500\"` to `\"599\"`, so every 5xx code counts. It was created enabled, so I didn't need to enable it afterwards. It has no actions attached, so it records alerts but doesn't notify anyone.\n4. **Panel edit and read-back:**\n   - The response-code panel is now titled 'Responses by code'. I re-read the dashboard and the panel titles are 'Requests over time' and 'Responses by code'.\n   - I read the rule back and it is enabled. Its last run showed status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
